= Leaders and their views

Every Senate bloc and every faction of the Caucus has a leader, and leaders change with the times. A Senate bloc's leader speaks for its ruling wing and is chosen by its congress (see *Parties and their wings*). A neglected Rim turns to radicals; a frightened Senate turns to hardliners. Inside the Caucus, a faction's leader can be challenged when the galaxy pushes hard enough, and the Caucus can stay out, use an advisor, or use the leadership's weight.

**What a leader changes**

• *Firmness.* Hardliners make their bloc vote as one, hold grudges, and are hard to court. Pragmatists swing with good relations and forgive quickly. Inside the Caucus, a firm faction leader makes the faction quicker to anger and quicker to break away; a pragmatist makes it patient.

• *Views.* Each leader leans for or against Rim reform, regulating the guilds, emergency powers, the army, Senate oversight, peace talks and no-confidence votes, and their bloc votes accordingly.

• *Partners.* Some leaders refuse to govern alongside certain blocs, which can rule out a coalition entirely.

• *Demands.* Each Caucus faction leader has their own demands, shown in the Status panel when they are not met.

• *The galaxy.* Radical Rim leaders feed separatism, and a Banking Clan hardliner pushes the guilds toward the Confederacy. Pragmatists cool both.

**Moods and memory**

• *What they care about.* Every leader has a motive and a list of things that please or anger them: tariffs, Rim relief, the war, emergency powers, who sits in government. The sidebar shows each leader's current stance and the main reason for it.

• *Anger hardens, goodwill softens.* An angry leader holds their bloc's line harder: in votes, in coalition talks and in how long they bear a grudge. A pleased leader bends. Inside the Caucus, each wing puts up its strongest figure at every congress.

• *Memory.* Leaders remember whether we backed them or their rival at a congress or in a leadership fight, whether we met or refused their demands, and whether their bloc walked out of our government. Memories fade over a few years.

• *Ultimatums and offers.* A leader who is angry and on poor terms with the Caucus will make a demand: the guilds want tariffs or regulation dropped, the Rim wants relief, the Security Bloc wants the fleet funded, the Moderates want reform slowed, the unaligned worlds want exemptions. Meeting it earns their favour; refusing it earns a grudge and a cost. A pleased leader on good terms offers help instead.

**The leaders today**

[+ leaders_html +]

**Who rises, and when**

• *The Senate's blocs.* Each bloc's leader comes from its ruling wing. The *Parties and their wings* page lists every wing, what grows it, and who leads it. Tal Ardana of the Rim Radicals will not govern with the Core Moderates, the guilds or the Security Bloc, and stokes separatism.

• *Our wings.* Each wing starts under a mainstream leader, and at each congress puts up whichever of its figures the times favour. Corruption and emergency powers raise a firm Institutionalist, Chi Eekway Papanoida. Rim hardship raises a radical Rimward leader, Jessa Corvane. War-weariness raises an absolutist Pacifist, Tamra Shen. Fear and defeats raise a militarist Hawk, Darra Voss. When a wing's cause is going well, its pragmatists rise: Terr Taneel, Hollin Rusk, Orrin Dask and Talia Morn.

@lib_wings
is-special: true
