= Parties and their wings

Every bloc in the Senate is a coalition of wings. Each wing has an ideology, a figure who leads it, regions where it is strongest, and conditions that make it grow. The ruling wing's leader leads the bloc, and the bloc is strongest in the ruling wing's home regions. A bloc's congress meets every four years, and early in a crisis; the wing with most support wins, after a second round of bargaining if no wing has a majority. A routine congress the ruling wing dominates re-elects its leader without a contest. Some delegates belong to no wing.

**The Core Moderates**

• *Ruusan Republicans* (Finis Valorum; Fang Zar once he arrives; Valorum can return a year after his fall). Clean government and slow reform within the old rules. They rule at the start, and are the only Moderate wing that puts reforms forward. They lose delegates if the Caucus pursues Valorum's shipping scandal or joins the vote of no confidence against him, and grow as the Caucus tackles High Human Culture and gives the Rim its seats. Strongest in the Colonies.

• *Core Establishment* (Davan Quill). Order and trade, and change only if necessary. Grow with fear and with the pace of reform. Strongest in the Core.

• *Old Families* (Tessaly Doran of Corulag). Humancentric, traditionalist, receptive to Palpatine. Grow with the High Human Culture, fear and Palpatine's standing; shrink once High Human Culture is discredited. Strongest of all in the Core.

**The Commerce Guilds**

• *Corporatists* (Lott Dod). The Trade Federation's way. Weakened by the invasion of Naboo, by the trials of Viceroy Gunray, and above all by a conviction of Gunray or Dod. Strongest in the Corporate holdings and the Mid Rim.

• *Confederates* (San Hill). The Techno Union and the Banking Clan, who see the Republic as a broken, humancentric state that means to seize their wealth. Grow with taxes and regulation on the guilds, with the High Human Culture, and with Dooku's movement. Strongest in the Corporate holdings.

• *Free Traders* (Rush Clovis, from 25 BBY). Commerce inside a lawful Republic. They start small and are hard to bring to power, but grow with each round of deregulation, the Anti-Cartel Act, the Trade Federation's break-up, the pirates crushed and the syndicates broken. Strongest in the Colonies and the Mid Rim.

• *Military-Industrial Complex* (Aldo Brask of the Fondor yards). The yards and foundries that profit from the war. They appear only once the war begins or the Corporate holdings have largely seceded, grow with the war's length, the clone army and the debt, and shrink with peace talks. If the Confederates and Corporatists leave, they inherit the guilds. Strongest in the Core and the Colonies.

**The Rimward Coalition**

• *Hutt-space Machine* (Orn Free Taa). Deals, patronage and the cartels. Leads the Coalition at the start. Grow with corruption and the Hutt deal, and shrink with the slavery ban and the ethics law; breaking the syndicates guts them. Strongest in the Outer Rim.

• *Rim Populists* (Ainlee Teem). A place for the Rim inside the Republic: an end to humancentrism and slavery, and seats for the Rim. They start small, and grow as the Rim's grievances are answered: the Rim Relief Act, the Rim's seats, the slavery report and ban, the Rights of Sentience, corruption below 50 and less hardship. Strongest in the Mid and Outer Rim.

• *Rim Radicals* (Tal Ardana). Reform now: they no longer believe the Republic can change, and many listen to Dooku. Grow when the Rim suffers or its worlds secede, and fade once the Rim is enfranchised. Strongest in the Outer Rim.

**The Security Bloc**

• *Law and Order Moderates* (Orvos Hask). Broadly moderate: order, a Republic that pays for its defence, and lanes cleared of pirates. Strongest in the Colonies.

• *Human-Firsters* (Wilhuff Tarkin, once he has made his name). Order through force, and the High Human Culture. Grow with fear, a war going badly and the High Human Culture. Strongest in the Outer Rim's human worlds and the Core.

• *Constitutional Hawks* (Giddean Danu, from 30 BBY). A strong defence under the Senate's control. Grow when the Senate reasserts itself. Strongest in the Core and the Colonies.

**The Independents**

• *Brokers* (Tamsar Vell). A negotiated end to the war, loyalty to the Republic once it fights, and a reformed Republic. A reforming wing. Strongest in the Mid Rim.

• *Neutralists* (Satine Kryze, once Dooku's movement or the war begins). Mandalore's peace. A reforming wing. Grow with war-weariness and peace talks. Strongest in the Outer Rim.

• *Isolationists* (Corwin Shale). Leave the member worlds alone: seats and rights for the Rim, but no new taxes and no new powers for Coruscant. Grow when money is short and the Rim suffers.

**The Rim Freedom Caucus** (if our Rimward wing breaks away)

• *Pragmatists* (Onaconda Farr) and *Radicals* (Mira Tandeth).

**Palpatine's bloc**

While he is free, Palpatine leads his bloc whatever its congress decides. The congress chooses the wing that sets its line, and that wing's figure becomes his lieutenant. If he falls, it chooses his successor.

• *Centralists* (Mas Amedda, or Sate Pestage after Amedda falls). His Chommell machine: a strong executive to restore order, clean up the Senate and renew the Republic. The largest wing at first, but their hold weakens year by year. Strongest in the Core and the Mid Rim.

• *New Order* (Janus Greejatus). A totalitarian, human Republic. Grow year by year, fastest while Palpatine is kept from the Chancellery, with the High Human Culture, and in reaction to Rim enfranchisement, the Rim Assembly and the Rights of Sentience; discrediting the High Human Culture holds them back. Out of office, Palpatine's bloc usually follows their line by the time of the war. Strongest in the Core.

• *Personality Cult* (Ars Dangor). Devotion to Palpatine himself. Grow year by year, and surge once he holds the Chancellery and with each new emergency power; with him in office they usually lead his bloc. Collapse if he is unmasked or disgraced. Strongest in the Core.

**What the ruling wing changes**

Every bloc votes by its ruling wing's views as well as its leader's. The wings differ on slavery, the Rights of Sentience, the Rim's seats, corruption, centralising the Republic, the constitution, command of the army and trade-route taxes, and some do more:

• *Old Families.* The Moderates warm to Palpatine and follow his line on emergency powers, and the human-first movement grows bolder.

• *Corporatists.* Against trade-route taxes and anti-corruption laws, for the Rights of Sentience and constitutional reform. They warm to a Caucus led by its Pacifists.

• *Confederates.* The guilds lose patience faster and regain it slowly. If their escalation reaches its last step after Dooku's declaration, the Confederates and the Corporatists walk out to the Separatists.

• *Free Traders.* For anti-corruption laws and a strong navy. The guilds stop escalating.

• *Military-Industrial Complex.* For a strong Chancellor, the army and war spending; against peace talks, constitutional reform and Senate command of the army. The guilds stop escalating, the clone army grows faster (or the Judicial Forces, without one), corruption rises, and the guilds warm to Palpatine.

• *Hutt-space Machine.* While the Caucus governs, it offers terms: leave slavery alone, put no new rules on the guilds, and stay out of the Rim's affairs. While the terms hold, it offers evidence from its spies, political capital, or the Rimward Coalition in our government. Refusing or breaking the terms turns it to Palpatine, and the Hutts strike back: bounties, cartel money for our rivals, relief blocked in Hutt space, smears. Breaking the syndicates guts the Machine and ends it all.

• *Rim Radicals.* They vote against the government's business except on the Rim, slavery and the Rights of Sentience, and every other government bill loses ground. Each reform on the Rim, slavery or the High Human Culture eases the obstruction and costs them delegates. While they rule, the Rimward Coalition's defection to the Separatists needs warm relations or two new reforms to avert, or a congress that removes them.

• *Law and Order Moderates.* Each step against the pirates and syndicates, and a defence budget, wins them; unchecked raids lose them.

• *Human-Firsters.* In the last days the Security Bloc's forces stand with Palpatine, and it votes for his New Order as one.

• *Constitutional Hawks.* For oversight and Senate command of the army. In the last days the Caucus can call on them once: Kuat's defence fleets join our side and the Security Bloc votes against the New Order. No admirals strike while they rule.

• *Brokers.* For peace talks, but behind the Republic once it fights. *Neutralists.* The Federal Compact comes easier. *Isolationists.* The Independents' ministries put forward only the Rim's seats and the Rights of Sentience.

• *Farr's Pragmatists.* If the Rim's reforms are complete (relief, seats and the Rim Assembly) and slavery has been abolished and enforced, the Rim Freedom Caucus rejoins the Caucus.

• *Palpatine's bloc.* The Centralists can back anti-corruption laws, draw the Moderates toward him, and in the last days purge his critics and win over the Moderates and Independents for his New Order. The New Order sends human militias into the streets, tries to repeal the Rim's rights once he holds the Chancellery, and brings the militias into his forces at the end. The Personality Cult votes down reforms and fills the plazas with crowds in the last days. Whichever wing leads his bloc shapes the Empire, if he wins.

**Radical wings**

A wing turns radical when its own bloc backs what it opposes. Each time the bloc's senators mostly vote for a bill, every wing whose own views (and its figure's) run against the bill's topics grows more radical, more so the more firmly the bloc voted. The same goes for a budget's taxes and spending (a levy on the Core, tariffs, Rim relief, defence, subsidies), for the bloc's own ministers acting against a wing, and for each season in government beside a partner the wing rejects. Radicalism fades by about a twelfth each season, and halves when the wing wins its bloc's congress.

• *Restless* (35): angrier at a lost congress, and ready to walk out even when closer to the winner.

• *Furious* (65): angrier still, and ready to walk out with as little as an eighth of the delegates. The galaxy hears of it.

• *On the brink* (80): if it has somewhere to go, it forces its bloc into an emergency congress, at most once in eighteen months and never in the year and a half after a congress.

Radicalism never wins a wing delegates; it is about splitting, not taking over.

**Others' money at congresses**

• *Palpatine's agitators* fan the grievances of the wing whose walkout would most serve him or Dooku. The more the Caucus reforms, the likelier he is to send them. They can be foreseen and exposed.

• *Bought delegates.* When Palpatine buys a leadership fight, his money goes to the hardline wings of the bloc he chose, preferring a radical one.

• *The guilds' money.* When the guilds' patience is below 35, and they are not led by the Free Traders or the war industry, the Trade Federation and the Banking Clan back the wing most opposed to regulation, trade taxes and the ethics laws at other blocs' congresses: six points of delegates, as our own backing gives. A wing they paid for that loses turns more radical.

**Repeals**

A bloc that chairs a committee can put a reform's repeal to the Senate when its ideology favours it over anything else the committee could do. It must be the latest step of its reform path in force, and must have stood for a year. The Caucus votes against, and blocs friendly to us, faith in the Republic and the Senate's resolve count against it. Repealable: the Rim Relief Act and the Rights of Sentience (Rim Affairs), the Ethics Commission and the Office of Public Integrity (Judiciary), the Sunset Amendment (Constitutional Affairs, by two thirds) and the Anti-Cartel Act (Finance). Reforms whose effects cannot be undone, and those that check the Chancellery's powers, are never repealed. A repealed reform can be passed again.

**Walkouts**

After a congress, a defeated wing with at least a fifth of the delegates, far from the winner in ideology and angry, may walk out. It is angry because it lost, and angrier if its bloc sits in government beside partners it rejects, if we backed its rival, if the gulf is very wide, or if it has turned radical. Each walkout takes the wing's share of its bloc's support with it.

• *Old Families*, *Human-Firsters*, the *Hutt-space Machine* and the *Military-Industrial Complex* join Palpatine's bloc.

• *Law and Order Moderates* rarely walk out; if they do, they join the Core Moderates.

• *Ruusan Republicans* join the Ruusan Caucus, bringing us seats and strengthening our Institutionalists.

• *Core Establishment* rarely walk out; if they do, they join the Security Bloc.

• *Corporatists*, *Confederates*, *Rim Radicals*, the Rim Freedom Caucus's *Radicals* and the *Isolationists* go over to the Separatists, once Dooku's movement exists.

• *Free Traders* found the Free Commerce League.

• *Constitutional Hawks* join us if our Hawks lead the Caucus; otherwise they found the Constitutional Defence League.

• *Neutralists* join us if our Pacifists lead the Caucus; otherwise they found the Council of Neutral Systems.

A new party takes the breakaway slot the Rim Freedom Caucus would use, and votes and governs as its old bloc would, by its own wing's views. Only one can exist at a time. If the slot is taken, a would-be founder joins the nearest bloc: the Free Traders and the Constitutional Hawks the Core Moderates, the Neutralists the Rimward Coalition.

@lib_meters
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The state of the Republic, explained

**Political capital** is the Caucus's currency. Patrons contribute each year, and most important actions spend it. A card none of whose options can be chosen (most often for want of capital) offers *Set this card aside*: the card is put away and the turn passes, so a hand of cards the Caucus cannot afford never leaves it stuck.

**Faith in the Republic** falls with corruption, fear and emergency powers, and rises with reform. Low faith feeds the Chancellor's bloc.

**Corruption** rises slowly by itself. It feeds Palpatine's appeal as a strong, clean leader, and it feeds separatism.

**Fear** rises with secession and war. It feeds the Security Bloc and the case for emergency powers.

**Outer Rim hardship** drives Rim separatism. Relief, enfranchisement and abolition reduce it. Guild credit strikes and war increase it.

**Separatism** in the Outer Rim, Mid Rim and corporate worlds decides how many worlds leave once Count Dooku's movement begins.

**Emergency powers** measure how much authority the Senate has handed the Chancellor. Each step on the ladder is a Senate vote.

**Evidence** is what the Caucus can prove about the Sith. Below *compelling*, accusations will backfire.

**Senate resolve** is how seriously the Senate takes the danger to its own authority. The Defence Pact, a no-confidence vote and the Petition all need it.

**In the corridors** is the only hint the game gives of the Sith Lord's hidden progress.

For how these numbers feed each other, see *How the systems interact*.

@lib_systems
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= How the systems interact

Every number in the Status panel feeds several others, so a problem left alone in one place spreads to the rest. When you play a card, the turn ends and the galaxy moves in this order:

• The calendar advances, and card and advisor cooldowns tick down.

• Our wings' strengths drift with what we have done, their radicalism fades, and relations with other blocs fade toward their natural level.

• Corruption, fear, faith, Rim hardship, separatism and the Sith's hidden plan update.

• Each bloc's support in each region drifts toward a natural level set by those numbers. Your campaigns raise the Caucus's own level in a region for years, fading slowly.

• Worlds with strong separatism secede. In wartime, the front moves.

• Events fire. Seats are recounted only every January and at Chancellor elections, so the polls run ahead of the seats.

Figures are per quarter. Half-year turns count double.

