@lib_bureaucrats
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= The bureaucrats

*"Enter the bureaucrats: the true rulers of the Republic. And on the payroll of the Trade Federation, I might add."* (Senator Palpatine)

The Senate's permanent staff of clerks, committee secretaries and procedural officers decide which bills reach the floor and which vanish in committee. They appear in **Our relations** on the Senate tab, like a bloc.

**What they like.** Governments of the establishment. When the Caucus joins or forms a government, the bureaucrats judge its partners at once: the Commerce Guilds, Palpatine's people and the Core Moderates reassure them; the Security Bloc and the Independents a little. The Caucus itself, the Rimward Coalition and breakaway reformers alarm them. The coalition choices tell you how they will react.

**What turns them.** While the Caucus governs or chairs committees, they cool every turn that its partners worry them, and faster when the Caucus polls well, passes reforms, or squeezes the guilds who pay them. An Ethics Commission and good relations with the guilds help.

**What they do.** When they turn **cold**, our reform bills lose support in committee. When they turn **hostile**, they prepare to move against us. While they are cold they also **build a case against us**, faster once they turn hostile; the Senate tab shows how far it has got (gathering files, half built, ready to strike). When the case is complete, or relations hit bottom, they strike. If the Caucus is a junior partner, a "reorganisation" throws it out of government and strips its chairs, and the Chancellor's bloc will not take it back for a year. Against a Caucus Chancellor they manufacture a crisis, the partners abandon the government, and it **falls**, as Valorum's did; the scandal follows our candidate into the next election. Only the constructive no-confidence amendment keeps a Caucus Chancellor in office.

**Winning them back.** Finis Valorum, Bail Organa and Bail Antilles can each work with the bureaucrats as advisors. The Judiciary & Intelligence Committee can reform the bureaucracy itself. A more establishment coalition, or easing off the guilds, reassures them.

**The payroll.** Once our evidence is strong enough, the Shadow tab shows where the bureaucracy's payroll leads.

@lib_schemes
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= Sidious's schemes

A hidden hand answers the Caucus's successes. Every few turns, someone moves against us: an ally poisoned, a smear on the HoloNet, a bomb on the eve of peace talks, a bloc leader bought, records erased, the Jedi framed, the guilds turned against us, the bureaucrats given new orders, Separatist agitators paid, senators lured across the floor.

**What draws its attention.** Whatever threatens it most. Strong evidence draws poisoners and archive thieves; a popular Caucus draws smears and defections; peace talks draw bombs; a suspicious Jedi Order draws forgeries. The more the Caucus achieves, the more often it happens.

**Seeing it coming.** If the Jedi are suspicious (4 or more), our evidence is strong (5 or more), or we have run a Shadow Investigation in the last two turns, the scheme is **foreseen** a turn early, and we can counter it with political capital or an advisor's time. A foiled scheme leaves a little evidence behind.

The Shadow tab lists the last three misfortunes.

@lib_war
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The War Council

When the war begins, the Senate sets its **war aims**. *Total victory* pushes the fronts forward but freezes the peace talks and keeps fear high; the Hawks like it. *A negotiated peace* is slower on the battlefield but grows the talks; the Pacifists like it. *The liberation of the Rim* favours the Outer Rim front and lets Rim reforms win seceded worlds back.

The war is fought on **three fronts**, shown on the Forces tab. Each one feeds a different part of the galaxy:

• *The Outer Rim Sieges.* Losing ground here means hunger and separatism on the Rim; gaining ground eases both.

• *The Hydian Way and the Mid Rim.* This is the Republic's trade road. Losing it strangles trade, and with it the budget.

• *The Core approaches.* Raids on the Core bring fear to Coruscant, and fear feeds the Chancellor's powers. Holding them calms the Senate.

Every second turn, the **War Council** meets. It reports the season's battle, fought where the Confederacy chose to strike, then sets a priority: one front gains at the expense of the other two, or the Republic holds everywhere at a cost. If the Caucus holds the Chancellery or the Security & Defence chair, it decides. Otherwise the Chancellor's council decides (Palpatine always protects the Core and keeps the war going), and the Caucus can only lobby.

Each council also faces a **dilemma** from the war: General Krell's orders on Umbara, arming Cham Syndulla's Free Ryloth fighters, blockades and humanitarian corridors, Mandalore's neutrality, the member-world fleets, a clone surge from Kamino, refugees, the Zygerrian slavers, the overstretched Jedi, Tarkin's Citadel, the bacta convoys and the Hydian Way convoys. Each choice reaches beyond the battlefield: into the Jedi's standing, Anakin's heart, the Rim's loyalty, the budget, the markets and the Sith's plan. Without command, the Caucus's choice needs a Senate majority.

When a front reaches **breaking point**, worlds change sides: a collapse loses systems to the Confederacy, and a breakthrough brings seceded worlds home. A Confederacy stronger than the Republic also takes **named loyalist worlds**: see *The Separatist threat*.

@lib_purse
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= The Republic's purse

The Economy tab shows the treasury, the Republic's debt to the InterGalactic Banking Clan, the health of hyperlane trade, and three markets.

**The budget.** Once a year, after the Senate's new session, the Republic passes a budget. Whoever holds the Chancellery or the Finance & Commerce chair writes it; otherwise the Caucus can support the government's budget, vote against it, or, in government, pay to change its priority. **Every budget must pass the Senate.** Each bloc judges what it costs its own worlds: the guilds vote against tariffs, the Core Moderates against the levy, the Rim against cuts. Coalition partners object to lines that cross their red lines, which strains the coalition. A defeated budget means a stopgap with no new revenue and no priority. Two of our own budgets defeated in a row bring down a Caucus Chancellor, unless the constructive no-confidence amendment protects them.

**Every tax has a price.** A budget raises money one way: trade-route tariffs, a levy on the Core worlds, a loan from the Banking Clan, war bonds, debasing the Republic credit, or, once legalised, a spice duty. Each has people who pay for it, and they push back **harder every year it is used in a row**:

• *Tariffs* wear down the guilds' patience, and shippers pass the cost on to the Rim.

• *The Core levy* falls on the Caucus's own patrons in Alderaan, Chandrila and Corellia. Their goodwill sets how much political capital we raise each year, and if it collapses, the Core revolts.

• *Loans* please the guilds but grow the debt.

• *War bonds* only sell when faith in the Republic is high; a failed bond drive is a public humiliation.

• *Debasing the credit* raises the most money and causes inflation: prices rise, the Rim suffers first, Core savers turn on us, and eventually Rim worlds stop accepting Republic credits at all.

• *Spending* has losers too: relief annoys the Core, subsidies anger the Rimward wing, defence the Pacifists, and cutting relief after funding it makes the Rim feel betrayed. Relief also earns the Caucus small donations from Rim worlds.

**The guilds' patience.** The Trade Federation once blockaded Naboo over a tax on trade routes, and the guilds still know how to fight. Tariffs, regulation, audits, seizures and the Federation's break-up wear their patience down; subsidies, loans, lifted sanctions and concessions rebuild it, and it returns slowly when the pressure stops. As it falls they go further, step by step: lobbying against our bills, a shipping slowdown, a credit strike, a blockade of a Caucus-friendly world, and finally defection to the Separatists. The Economy tab shows what angered them and what comes next.

**Money is politics.** Angry guilds, and creditors owed a fortune, bankroll our rivals in Chancellor elections and fund HoloNet campaigns that wear down our support. Pleased guilds fund us instead, for a price. Angry Core patrons keep their purses shut.

**Debt.** Any shortfall, in the budget or during the year, is lent by the Banking Clan. Interest grows with the debt. A heavy debt lets the Banking Clan name its price; a crushing one forces a choice between austerity and a bailout that makes the banks, and whoever stands behind them, stronger. It also gives a Chancellor who wants the banks an excuse to seize them. The war is expensive, and wars are how republics fall into debt.

**Trade.** Hyperlane trade feeds the budget. Piracy, the guild embargo, Rim hardship and the war on the Hydian Way choke it; patrols, Rim representation and peace revive it. When trade is strangled, hardship on the Rim grows.

**Markets.** *Bacta* from Thyferra runs short when the war fills the field hospitals. *Tibanna gas* from Bespin, which powers blasters and hyperdrives, grows dear as the Republic arms. *Spice* from Kessel flourishes when the Rim suffers and the Senate is corrupt, and the Pyke Syndicate and the Hutts fight over it. When a market reaches crisis, the Senate must choose who pays: the treasury, the guilds, the front, or the poor. The Finance & Commerce Committee can act early: patrols, bacta stockpiles, and refinancing the debt.

@lib_elections
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= Elections and campaigns

Senators answer to their home worlds, and the Caucus's fortunes rise and fall with the galaxy's mood.

**Momentum.** The Polls tab shows whether the Caucus is surging, rising, steady, falling or collapsing, and why. Momentum lifts our support in every region and sways every bloc at a Chancellor election. Reforms, strong campaigns, debates, endorsements and won contests build it; scandals, gaffes, broken promises, lost contests and broken-up rallies drain it. It fades each quarter unless renewed.

**Campaigning.** A campaign picks a region and then a message. *Clean government* lands when the Senate is corrupt, best in the Core and Colonies, and falls flat right after a Caucus scandal. *Relief for the Rim* lands in the Mid and Outer Rim when hardship is high, and is only believed if the Caucus has funded relief. *Security* lands when the galaxy is afraid, best in the Colonies; the Pacifists dislike it. *Peace* lands with a war-weary galaxy but **backfires** when fear runs high; the Hawks dislike it. *The Republic and its laws* is steady everywhere. Each option shows how well it is expected to land. What a campaign wins in a region lasts for years, fading slowly, and campaigning there again builds on it, each time adding a little less.

**Election season.** In the last year before a scheduled Chancellor election, the Caucus can campaign every turn, and (from the second election on) the campaign trail brings HoloNet debates, endorsements (Duchess Satine, the Mon Calamari, Kuat Drive Yards; the Jedi Order stays out of politics), opposition research, gaffes and smears. The Polls tab shows our whip count.

**The Chancellor race.** If the Caucus has the seats to stand, it chooses its **nominee**: each of our leading figures appeals to different blocs, and Padmé Amidala takes Naboo's sympathy vote away from Palpatine. Rival candidates have strengths and weaknesses of their own: the Rim's populists are distrusted in the Core, the Security Bloc gains when the galaxy is afraid, and when the Senate is corrupt, Palpatine's backers quietly buy votes. With strong evidence (4 or more) the Caucus can **expose the money**.

**The whip count** shows how each bloc is leaning, and why. It is reliable with the Judiciary chair, strong evidence, or Bail Organa or Finis Valorum as an advisor; otherwise it may be several points out. After the vote, the Caucus learns how far off it was, and which blocs broke away.

**Pledges.** A pledge wins a bloc outright: no trade-route tariffs (the guilds), no levy on the Core (the Moderates), Rim relief in every budget (the Rim), defence spending in every budget (the Security Bloc), no new emergency powers (the Independents). They are made only in a Chancellor race, and bind the Caucus until the next one, in government or not. Budgets and votes warn before we break one. A broken pledge costs that bloc's goodwill, strains a coalition, drains momentum, and its leader remembers.

**Between the ballots.** If no one wins a majority, the two leaders go to a second ballot and the corridors become a marketplace. If our candidate is still in, we can court the eliminated bloc or promise it a pledge. If our candidate is out, our votes may decide the race: either finalist will trade a committee chair and a place in government for them.

**Coalition talks.** A partner with a firm or hardline leader insists on a committee chair of its own (the guilds Finance, the Moderates Judiciary, the Rimward Coalition Rim Affairs, the Security Bloc Security & Defence, Palpatine's bloc Judiciary); a pragmatic one settles for a fair share. Promised chairs cannot be bought. Partners never ask for promises, and joining someone else's government costs none: the only pledges that bind the Caucus are the ones it makes in a Chancellor race.

**Minority governments.** A Caucus Chancellor can govern as a minority: no partner insists on a chair, so the Caucus can buy more of them. In exchange it leans on the blocs outside the government whose votes it needs, the friendliest first, until together they hold a majority (the Senate tab marks them ☆). Their patience counts as the coalition's mood, and it wears each quarter just as partners' would, faster with firmer leaders, and faster still if no bloc will support the government at all. Every one of the government's own bills that wins less than half the Senate adds a full point; the budget has its own price, and a constitutional amendment that wins a majority but not two-thirds does not count. A minority starts *strained* and falls at *collapsing*, one step sooner than a coalition: the least friendly of its partners and supporters leaves, a no-confidence vote follows, and that bloc will not support or serve with the Caucus for two years. Under the constructive no-confidence amendment the Chancellor survives, and the government leans on whoever is left.

**A fresh mandate.** A Caucus Chancellor whose coalition is restless, or whose budget has been defeated, can call a confidence vote once a term. Win, and the term starts afresh with a calm coalition (it counts as a new term); lose, and the government falls.

**World and sector elections.** Elections across the galaxy matter on Coruscant: the Malastare sector, Mon Mothma's mandate on Chandrila, Naboo's royal election, the Corellian Diet, Onaconda Farr's Rodia, the Kuat succession, and in wartime Ryloth and Mandalore. Each moves delegations and momentum and has its own stakes: Corellia's fleet, Palpatine's home base, Mandalore's neutrality. An advisor campaigning on home ground is the strongest help. A lost seat sends Mothma or Bel Iblis home for a year.

**Referendums.** Once Dooku's movement is under way, a world in a region where separatism runs high may vote on leaving. The Caucus can promise relief, send a figure (Lux Bonteri, on Onderon), expose the Separatist money, offer autonomy, or campaign. A world that leaves takes its delegations with it; one that stays calms its whole region.

**Rallies under attack.** Any campaign can be stormed: by Nebula Front militants early on, Separatist agitators, thugs hired by angry guilds, bounty hunters in wartime, and Death Watch on the Outer Rim. The Caucus can call the Judicial Forces, ask a reformed Senate Guard, rely on local security (strong where we are popular), or cancel. If protection fails, the rally breaks up, fear rises, and we lose ground. Leaning on armed force too often troubles the Pacifists.

**Recalls.** A region builds a grievance when the Caucus backs measures that hurt it: a levy on the Core, a debased credit, cuts to Rim relief, tariffs on the corporate worlds, or budgets that starve a war. Grievances fade with time; the Polls tab marks aggrieved regions. When one boils over, its worlds recall Caucus senators.

@lib_endings
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= How the game ends

The game ends in 19 BBY, after the Last Days, unless it ends sooner.

**Endings must be earned.** Surviving to the end is not enough. If Darth Sidious has not been unmasked and stopped, he proclaims the Empire, unless his powers have been stripped to 2 or fewer, the evidence against him is public, and term limits are entrenched, in which case the Republic endures with a shadow over it.

**The roads' endings.** If the Sith Lord is defeated and the Republic Reborn is out of reach, a completed road gives its own ending instead of *The Republic Endures*: *The Senate's Sword* (taken by the Senate's own forces), *The Second Reformation* (the Charter ratified), *The Republic Whole* (the Rim brought home) or *The Long Peace* (the armies stood down).

**The best ending**, *The Republic Reborn*, needs all of these: Sidious defeated and tried (not killed), the Restoration Act passed, faith of 50 or more, corruption under control (below 65, or an Ethics Commission), and at least one lasting safeguard in place.

**The arrest** needs preparation: see *How the systems interact*. A raid without a warrant, a loyal Senate Guard, Judicial Forces, conclusive evidence and a strong Jedi Order usually fails. Only a warrant prepared beforehand (Judiciary & Intelligence) counts: there is no rushing one through in the Last Days.

**The Last Days only draw on what was built.** Each Last Days card needs its groundwork:
• *Mobilise the Free Worlds* needs the Defence Pact.
• *Appeal to the Navy* needs earlier work with its officers: vetting them on Security & Defence, a diversified officer corps, or the Senate Command Act. Each piece of that work adds to its effect.
• *Rally the Delegation* needs the Delegation of 2000.
• *Secure the Evidence* needs a case already made; it stops the evidence from wasting away, but adds none.
• *Coordinate with the Jedi Council* needs close relations with the Order, and only moves the Council's suspicion if we have strong evidence to show them.
• *Reach Out to Anakin* needs someone close to him: Padmé, friends among the Jedi, or a living Qui-Gon.
An unreformed Senate Guard answers to the Chancellery and cannot be won over at the last. When Palpatine makes his move by decree, by coup or once exposed, the Defence Pact can only be mobilised if it exists, and the army only if the Senate oversees it, has removed its chips or commands it.

**A war still raging at the end** decides the ending, whether or not Sidious has fallen. It is judged on everything: the front, the two sides' strength and the worlds already taken (the Forces tab sums it up as being won, in the balance or being lost).

• *The Fall of the Republic.* The war is being lost when the game ends, or a civil war is fought while it is being lost, or the Confederacy takes Coruscant. Capturing or killing Sidious does not save a Republic that is losing its war.

• *The Sith Confederacy.* Sidious escaped to the Confederacy, and the Republic was not clearly winning the war.

• *Confederacy Triumphant.* A Republic well behind, but not crushed, negotiates its own defeat.

• *The Endless War.* Neither side can win.

Only Sidious's fall with the war going well lets it wind down.

**The game can be lost early:**

• *The New Order, early.* If Palpatine is Chancellor with 8 emergency powers and the Senate's resolve is below 5, the next crisis lets him proclaim the Empire. A warning comes at 7 powers.

• *The Senatorial Arrests.* If the Delegation of 2,000 defies a Chancellor who holds the Security Act and 6 powers, he moves against its signatories. A warning comes first; softening the Delegation, scattering the leadership, Senate resolve of 5 or a loyal Navy can prevent the worst.

• *The Admirals Move.* If fear reaches 60, Wilhuff Tarkin leads the Security Bloc and the Navy's loyalty to the Senate is below 50%, the admirals may seize power. A warning comes first; a reformed Senate Guard or a loyal Navy stops them.

• *The Fall of the Republic.* If the front collapses to −9.5, or the Separatists take every world on the road to the Core.

• *The admirals strike.* Even after Sidious is beaten in the Last Days, admirals with a Navy that does not answer to the Senate may take power, while High Human Culture has not been discredited and the Chancellery still holds emergency powers.

@lib_sys_corruption
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= Corruption, faith and fear

**Corruption** (starts at 60) is the most connected number in the game. It rises by about 0.3 a quarter on its own, and faster for each emergency power the Chancellor holds, during the war, and when the Commerce Guilds are strong in the Senate. Prosecutions, exposés and breaking up the Trade Federation knock it down once. Only a **Senate Ethics Commission** reverses the trend, taking off 0.6 every quarter.

Corruption lowers faith and shrinks the Core Moderates. It grows Palpatine's bloc by 4% for each point above 55, so his natural support doubles at 80. It feeds Rim and corporate grievances, speeds the Sith's hidden plan, and tilts Chancellor elections toward Palpatine. At 62, scandals begin and the Institutionalists grow restless. The best ending needs corruption below 65, or an Ethics Commission.

**Faith in the Republic** (starts at 50) drifts toward a target rather than moving directly. Corruption, fear, war-weariness and emergency powers lower the target. Each safeguard you pass raises it by 2.5, and so does a larger Caucus. Speeches and good news lift faith for a while, but only a better target makes the change last. Faith grows the Caucus, makes every bloc likelier to vote for reform, and decides a sitting Chancellor's re-election. The best ending needs 50.

**Fear** (starts at 20) rises fast in a crisis and fades fast once it passes. Dooku's movement, the war, defeats at the front and each wave of secession raise its target. Fear grows Palpatine's bloc and the Security Bloc, lowers faith, speeds the Sith's plan, and makes the Senate readier to hand out emergency powers.

@lib_sys_rim
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= Rim hardship, the guilds and secession

**Outer Rim hardship** (starts at 30) creeps up by itself, and faster during guild credit strikes and the war. Each Rim relief programme, the abolition of slavery and Rim enfranchisement slow it for good. Hardship grows the Rimward Coalition and feeds Rim separatism. At 38, pirate raids begin; at 55, famines. Above 40, the Caucus's Rimward wing grows restless unless the Rim is enfranchised.

**The guild embargo** is the guilds' willingness to squeeze the Republic. Taxing trade routes, regulating the guilds and breaking up the Trade Federation raise it. At 5 the guilds strike back, and at 8 they mount a full credit strike. The embargo raises Rim hardship and drains the treasury.

**Separatism** has two different sources. **Rim worlds** (the Outer and Mid Rim) leave over hardship and corruption, and separatism there is the default: it grows whenever Rim hardship is above 20 or corruption is above 40, and the further above, the faster it grows. Below those lines it eases back slowly. Enfranchisement, abolition, relief, the Ethics Commission and peace talks hold it back, and an enfranchised Rim slowly settles back toward loyalty, but only bringing hardship and corruption down stops the growth for good. **Corporate worlds** leave over how the guilds are treated: embargoes, the Trade Federation's break-up, trade taxes and poor relations with the guilds. Both kinds grow much faster once Dooku speaks on Raxus.

**The Rim radicalises.** Until Dooku's declaration, while hardship or corruption is over its line, news comes from the Rim every few turns: bread lines, strikes, scandals and bought votes, each told once, each with a crowd that has stopped asking Coruscant for anything. Each story raises separatism in its region (and a little in the other).

**The Rim's politicians radicalise too.** Each year from 28 BBY in which the Caucus has done nothing about slavery (a campaign, the Senate report or abolition) and has not enfranchised the Rim, the Rim's own politicians move a step further from the Republic. First they lose patience: our Rimward wing grows restless and the Rimward Coalition cools toward us. Then the radicals take over: Jessa Corvane leads our wing, Tal Ardana the Coalition (and Mira Tandeth the Rim Freedom Caucus, if our wing has split away), and moderates struggle to unseat them. Last, they look for a way out: once Dooku declares, they are ready to go over to him whatever the Rim's separatism, and only the usual remedies stop it (see *Shifting blocs and defections*). Any of those measures halts the slide, and each year after it the Rim's politicians step back one stage.

Once Dooku has spoken on Raxus, any region whose separatism is above 50 loses delegations every quarter. Each departure shrinks the Senate, raises fear, and leaves the Chancellor's bloc relatively larger. Secession brings the war sooner, but **Sidious runs both sides**: if nothing has started it by mid-22 BBY, the Confederacy is armed and the war begins anyway. Only the Federal Compact, or Rim enfranchisement together with real peace talks and a turned Count Dooku, can stop it. Reforms still decide how many worlds leave and how bad the war becomes.

@lib_sys_powers
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= Emergency powers and the Sith's plan

**Emergency powers** (0 to 10) measure how much authority the Senate has handed the Chancellor. Most steps are Senate votes you can contest: the term extension (24 BBY), Geonosis (22 BBY), powers "for the duration", the security act, the Banking Clan takeover, the sector governors, and Anakin's seat on the Council. Safeguards block steps outright. Entrenched term limits stop the extensions, banking independence protects the banks, Jedi independence protects the Council, and sunset clauses can stop the governors.

Each power adds corruption and lowers faith. Powers held by anyone but your own Chancellor also speed the Sith's plan, and at 4 or more they anger the Pacifists. A failed yearly sunset review takes two away, and after Sidious falls the Restoration Act can repeal them all. If 8 or more remain at the end, the Empire is born. If 5 or more survive Sidious's fall unrepealed, the admirals keep them. If your own Chancellor keeps them, the result is the Hollow Crown, and so it is if a Chancellor of ours has issued 5 or more decrees and the office still holds 6 or more of its powers at the end (see *The Chancellery and its powers*).

**The Sith's plan** is hidden. Only *In the corridors*, on the Shadow tab, hints at it, and the hint changes every 20 points. It grows with corruption, fear, the war and other people's emergency powers, and much faster while Palpatine is Chancellor. It grows more slowly when he is out of office, and slowest of all under a Caucus Chancellor. Evidence, Senate resolve and a suspicious Jedi Council slow it. Decommissioning the clones or putting them under oversight sets it back.

As the plan advances, Palpatine's bloc grows, elections lean his way, your evidence goes stale faster, and leaks and assassinations become likelier. Past 70, he may have a second Order 66 ready even if he is arrested, unless the clones' chips have been removed.

@lib_sys_proof
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= Evidence and Senate resolve

**Evidence** (0 to 12) is what the Caucus can prove about the Sith. The richest sources are Gunray's trials, finding Kamino early, the Sifo-Dyas mystery, the Damask money trail, Fives and the chips, and a Count Dooku turned against his master. Shadow Investigation and intelligence reviews add a little at a time. Above 5, evidence goes stale each quarter, and faster as the Sith's plan advances; a free press slows the decay. Leaks, murdered investigators and failed charges cost you.

Evidence slows the Sith's plan, and it opens doors. At 2 you can share findings with the Jedi. At 3, warnings of overreach sound credible. At 6, a false flag can be exposed. At 7 you can lay charges, at a risk. At 8 the Senate can issue a warrant. At 10 the proof is conclusive. Evidence made public shrinks Palpatine's bloc and his appeal at elections.

**Senate resolve** (0 to 10) is how seriously the Senate takes the danger to its own authority. Confronting the Chancellery, holding the term limit, Mon Mothma's warnings and the Chancellor's own overreach raise it. Failed charges and failed no-confidence votes lower it. Every point makes reform votes easier and slows the Sith's plan. Resolve works like a set of keys:

• 2: at Geonosis, the clones can deploy under Senate command.

• 3: the Free Worlds Defence Pact, the Petition of 2000, and a vote of no confidence in the Chancellor.

• 4: sheltering Fives.

• 5: the Delegation of 2000 forms.

@lib_sys_senate
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= Seats, elections and relations

Every bloc's support in every region drifts toward a natural level. Faith grows the Caucus, and a divided Caucus shrinks it. Low corruption grows the Core Moderates. Palpatine's bloc grows a little every year whatever happens, and corruption, fear, the Sith's plan and holding office make it grow faster. Fear and the war grow the Security Bloc, and Rim hardship grows the Rimward Coalition. Campaigns push support away from these levels for a while.

**Chancellor elections.** Each bloc backs the candidate it likes most: its own leader, an endorsed ally, or the candidate it leans toward, weighed by how strong that candidate's bloc really is. Without a majority, the top two go to a second ballot, and the Caucus can bargain between the ballots. Capital spent courting a bloc buys a large lean toward your candidate; a pledge buys a larger one, at a price later. The nominee, the rivals' strengths, momentum and money all count. In 32 BBY only, Naboo's plight gives Palpatine a sympathy vote. See *Elections and campaigns*.

**Incumbency.** Partners in a stable coalition back the sitting Chancellor instead of running against them. The Chancellor's record moves every bloc: faith, corruption, coalition harmony, and whether the war is going badly. **Coalition mood** worsens every turn the Caucus governs, faster with hardline partners and when we hold firm in a strain. When it reaches *collapsing*, the unhappiest partner walks out and the government falls. With constructive no-confidence, the Chancellor survives and the partner simply leaves, or, if the Caucus is the junior partner, the Caucus is dropped instead. A partner that walks out will not serve with the Caucus for two years.

**Votes.** Ordinary laws need half the Senate; constitutional amendments need **two-thirds**. While Palpatine's people control the Senate, the **Vice Chair** rules a Caucus reform out of order every two years or so, unless Senate resolve is 3 or more or the Senate Guard has been reformed. On votes about powers and oversight, **delegations on the Chancellery's payroll** follow its line; the more corrupt the Senate and the further the Sith's plan has gone, the more of them there are. If the **bureaucrats** turn cold, our bills also lose support in committee.

**Relations** with other blocs fade each quarter toward each bloc's natural attitude toward the Caucus, which depends on its leader. The Core Moderates are usually the friendliest; the guilds and the Separatists the coldest. Relations sway votes and elections, and they decide coalitions.

**Relations between the blocs.** Every pair of blocs has a relationship of its own, shown on the Senate tab: the Rim and the Core Moderates start cold, the Rim and the Security Bloc hostile, the Moderates and the guilds cordial. Each pair drifts toward a level set by what the two blocs stand for and what their leaders think of each other. Governing together warms a pair; running rival candidates for Chancellor cools it.

**Who will govern with whom.** No bloc will sit in a government beside a partner it is too cold toward, and each leader sets the bar: a hardliner needs cordial relations, a firm leader cool ones, and a flexible leader will sit even with a bloc they are cold toward (never a hostile one). A new Chancellor builds a coalition in order of their relationship with each party, the Caucus included, until it holds a majority. Every partner in a coalition the Caucus leads must clear the bar with us and with each other.

**A Senate that cannot govern.** If no coalition can reach a majority, or if a government falls apart (a coalition collapse, or the bureaucrats bringing it down), voters lose faith in the system itself: Palpatine's bloc gains across the galaxy, separatism rises on the Rim and the corporate worlds, and faith falls.

**Mending ties.** The Inter-bloc Relations card courts one bloc, brokers talks between two blocs whose coldness blocks a coalition we could lead, or hosts a summit for a whole coalition. Better still is to **deliver**: meeting a bloc leader's demand, passing a budget that funds what a bloc wants (relief for the Rim, defence for the Security Bloc, subsidies for the guilds and the Core, repayment for the Moderates and Independents), or keeping a pledge for a full term earns far more goodwill than any courtesy.

**Leaders.** A bloc's leader decides how firmly it holds its line, how it leans on each kind of vote, and which blocs it refuses to govern with. See *Leaders and their views*.

@lib_sys_caucus
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= Capital, wings and unity

**Political capital** arrives each January: 2 on Senator, 3 on Story, 1 on Jedi Master. You get 1 more if the Caucus is united (its wings' average radicalism below 8), 1 more while the Caucus sits in government, and 1 less if it is divided (above 25). Patron Contributions, some advisors and Routine Senate Business (only when you are nearly broke) add a little more. Courting blocs at elections, the Defence Pact, Rim enfranchisement, the Federal Compact and laying charges cost the most.

**Our wings** each have a strength, a radicalism and a leader (see *The wings of the Ruusan Caucus*). A divided Caucus shrinks most of its gains: its natural size, the reach of its campaigns and the yearly capital bonus. Calm a wing by giving its senators committee chairs, by meeting its cause, or with *A Divided Caucus*; a radical wing that loses our congress may walk out with its senators.

@lib_sys_war
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: lib_sys_menu

= The war, Anakin and the final reckoning

**The front** runs from −10 to +10. Sidious wants a long war, so while he rules the Republic, the front drifts toward a slow Republic advance if nobody pushes. Out of office, he no longer holds the droid armies back; once he is gone, the two sides' strength alone decides. Funding the front, Jedi generals and a turned Dooku push it forward. Every quarter of war drains the treasury, adds corruption, Rim hardship and war-weariness, and wears down the Jedi. The war ends with the Federal Compact, with victory at +9, or with a Treaty of Separation if the front is behind or weariness reaches 60. At −9.5, the Confederacy wins. If the game ends with the war still raging, the war as a whole decides: see *How the game ends*.

**Anakin falls unless something holds him.** Left alone, he goes the way he went in the films: from month 96 the dark side rises under him, and by month 168 he is as good as Palpatine's. Padmé, honesty from the Jedi, *Reach Out to Anakin* and every step against slavery still pull him back, but never below that rising line. Only three things lift it, and the *People* panel shows which one holds him, under Persons of interest:

• *His mother, free.* Enforcing abolition before Shmi's death in month 134 frees her.

• *A reformed Order.* The Council accepts attachment, or accepts his marriage when he is honest about it.

• *Friends who warned him*, which needs all three: the Jedi kept out of command (never made generals, or withdrawn when they are overstretched), a warning about Palpatine that was heard (*Palpatine's Protégé* when the Jedi trust us, *Ask about young Skywalker*, or telling him the truth while he still listens), and action on slavery (a campaign, the Senate report, the Zygerria rescue or abolition).

Once something holds him, he can be kept in the light, and an Anakin who chooses the light can help found a New Jedi Order, after which he will not fall. Shmi's death, Qui-Gon's death, Palpatine's mentorship, the Council seat and the war still push him toward the dark side.

**In the Last Days**, as in the film, Palpatine tells him the truth and Anakin goes to Windu, unless he already went over to Palpatine when the Caucus told him. At the arrest, an Anakin still in the light helps Windu; a fallen one strikes Windu down.

**The arrest has to be earned.** In its favour: Anakin in the light (+1.5), a reformed Senate Guard, a Senate warrant, Judicial Forces of 4+, conclusive evidence, a Jedi Order not worn down by the war (+1 each), exposing him first (+2), the Senate–Jedi pact (+0.5) and an informant (+1). Against it: a fallen Anakin (−2), a delay, and his plan. **The balance of forces counts too**: matching or outnumbering everyone who would answer to him gives +1; if he has a third more than us, −1; twice as much, −2; three times, −3. It needs 3.5, and a little luck plays its part. A raid without the groundwork usually fails, as Windu's did.

**The final reckoning.** If it comes to a fight, the game weighs everyone loyal to the Senate against everyone who would obey the Sith Lord. The Senate side has the Jedi (scaled by public trust), the Judicial Forces, the Defence Pact, the Senate Guard (if it was reformed or the Chancellery is yours), unchipped clones, a loyal Navy and Anakin. The Sith side has chipped clones, the Guard and the sector governors if his camp holds the Chancellery, a disloyal Navy, the Security Bloc's men, Black Sun's enforcers while the syndicates stand, and Vader. Clearly stronger means victory, roughly even means a long civil war, and much weaker means the Empire. Acting without a Senate warrant risks a Jedi Protectorate, even in victory.

@lib_chancellery
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= The Chancellery and its powers

The office of Supreme Chancellor stands apart from the six committees. It holds the Chancellor, the Vice Chair and whatever powers the office has gathered. The pinned *The Chancellery* card shows them all; looking is free.

**The twelve powers**, how each is gained, and the reform that checks it for good:
• *The gavel:* held from the start; the Vice Chair controls the agenda. Checked by an independent Speaker.
• *The Senate Guard:* held from the start; the Guard answers to the Chancellor. Checked by returning the Guard to the Senate.
• *The Chancellor's staff:* from 30 BBY, as the committees' staff move into the Chancellery (from 26 BBY if the Caucus resisted the transfers in *The Chancellery Grows*). Checked by the Civil Service Act.
• *Emergency decrees:* with the first emergency power. Sunset clauses suspend it; the Powers Review Act ends it.
• *The Grand Army:* once there is a clone army. Checked by Senate oversight of the army, or the Senate Command Act.
• *The Bureau of Intelligence:* *The Bureau Moves to the Chancellery*, at 2 or more emergency powers or after two years of war. Checked by the Intelligence Oversight Act, which also stops the move.
• *The HoloNet:* *The Office of Public Information*, at 3 or more emergency powers. Checked by a free press (an independent HoloNet foundation, from the Civil Society card), which also stops it.
• *Term without end:* the term extension or an indefinite term. Checked by entrenched term limits.
• *The Jedi Council:* the Chancellor's representative on the Council. Checked by the Jedi Independence Amendment.
• *The banks:* the Banking Clan's seizure. Checked by its independence, or Republic bonds.
• *The Security Act.* Checked by the Judicial Forces Charter.
• *The sector governors.* Checked by the Rim's constitutional seats, or the Rim Assembly.

**The scale.** 0 to 2 powers: *First among equals*. 3 to 4: *A strong executive*. 5 to 6: *A war cabinet*. 7 to 9: *An imperial presidency*. 10 or more: *The throne in all but name*.

**What the powers do.** Whoever holds the office, its own bills gain 1 point in every Senate vote for each power held, and 3 more while it holds the committees' staff. For our Chancellor these are the Caucus's bills; for Palpatine or his man, his bills for more power. Under Palpatine, his plan also gains 0.1 a quarter for each power. A Bureau in his hands wears away 0.1 of our evidence a quarter and raises his alarm.

**War in the Shadows.** When our Chancellor holds an office that holds the Bureau, the Bureau turns out to be split between loyal agents and a hidden network that answers to a darker master. *Purge* it (−1 political capital, −0.5 evidence, the bureaucrats angered) and the network is cut off at once. *Back the loyal agents* (+0.3 evidence) and it is rooted out over the next five quarters. *Leave it be* and it goes on raising the Sith Lord's alarm for as long as we hold the Bureau. Either way, the Bureau is ours to use afterwards.

**Rule by decree.** While our Chancellor holds the office and the office holds emergency decrees, each of these takes the turn:
• *Enact a reform by decree:* any committee step that would go to a Senate vote passes without one, if its card's own conditions are met. It costs what the card's option costs, and 3 faith.
• *The Guard arrests the corrupt* (the Senate Guard; −1 capital): corruption −4, fear +3.
• *The governors deliver relief* (the sector governors; −1 capital): Rim hardship −3.
• *Direct the Banking Clan* (the banks; −1 capital): debt −5.
• *Order the Grand Army forward* (the Grand Army, in wartime; −1 capital): the weakest front +1.5.
• *Address the galaxy* (the HoloNet; −1 capital): the Caucus's momentum +1.
• *Turn the Bureau on a rival bloc* (after *War in the Shadows*; −2 capital): the bloc's support falls 7% in every region, its relations with us fall by 6 and it holds a grudge, and its leader's hold on it weakens for a while.
• *Set the Bureau to investigate* (after *War in the Shadows*; −2 capital): +1 evidence and +0.5 to the Jedi's suspicion.

**The price of decrees.** Each decree adds 1 to the office's precedent. Each quarter, faith falls by 0.05 for each point. The Restoration Act loses about 2 points of support for each point. If Palpatine or his man later takes the office, his plan gains 2 for each point, once. And if, when the game ends, our Chancellor holds the office with a precedent of 5 or more and 6 or more powers still held, the ending is *The Hollow Crown*.

**Returning a power.** Our Chancellor can hand any held power back to the Senate (it takes the turn): the reform that checks it takes effect as if passed, faith rises by 3, and the precedent falls by 1.

**Other Chancellors** with emergency decrees rule by decree every four quarters, drawing on the powers the office holds: the Guard detains a Caucus senator, the HoloNet campaigns for the Chancellor's bloc, a governor breaks up a Rim protest, the Bureau watches the Caucus, or a decree takes one more emergency power. Each advances the Sith's plan, by 1 under Palpatine or his man and by 0.5 under anyone else.

@lib_reforms
is-special: true
go-to: menu

= Safeguards and how to win them

Some reforms permanently close doors that the Sith would otherwise walk through.

**Sunset clauses** (Constitutional Affairs): emergency powers expire unless renewed each year. **Term limits entrenched** (Constitutional Affairs): no Chancellor can have his term extended. **Senate oversight of the army** (Security & Defence, or at Kamino and Geonosis): the clones answer to the Senate too. Disbanding the clone army at Kamino counts as this safeguard: with no army there is nothing to oversee, no chips to remove and no Order 66, but the war must then be fought with the Judicial Forces and the member worlds. **Senate Guard reformed** (Security & Defence): the Red Guards stop answering to the Chancellor alone. **Jedi independence** (Jedi Affairs): the Chancellor cannot place the Council under his authority. **Banking independence** (Finance & Commerce): the Chancellery cannot seize the banks. **Rim enfranchisement** (Outer Rim Affairs or Constitutional Affairs): a lasting cure for separatism. **Ethics Commission** (Judiciary & Intelligence): a lasting check on corruption. **Inhibitor chips removed** (Clone Army Oversight, once found): Order 66 will fail.

Most of these need a seat in government and a Senate majority. Constitutional amendments need two-thirds.

Several of these safeguards are the first step of a longer reform: see *Reforms in steps*.

@lib_sith_search
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= The search for the Sith

The Caucus does not know who the Sith Lord is. What it knows (*the search*) and what it can prove (*our proof*, the evidence) are kept apart. The search never goes backwards.

**The stages.**

• *Rumours.* Something is wrong in the Republic.

• *A Sith presence.* The Jedi confirm a Sith Lord has returned (after Naboo), Gunray testifies, or the Jedi's suspicion reaches 3.

• *A hidden hand.* Proof of 3 or more, and a clue to the pattern behind the crises: the Damask trail, Gunray naming Darth Sidious, the guild probe or the Banking Clan audit, or the false-flag bombing exposed.

• *Close to power.* Proof of 5 or more, and a clue that leads to Coruscant: the Kamino payments, a Bureau intelligence review, Valorum's old networks, Gunray's word that Sidious knew the Senate's business, the Labyrinth raid, Dooku's warning at Geonosis passed on by friendly Jedi, or the Jedi's suspicion reaching 6.

• *A name.* Every suspect cleared, or Count Dooku turned.

**The shortlist.** Close to power, four people are suspected: Mas Amedda, the Vice Chair, who rigs the agenda for money and takes orders; Sate Pestage, a fixer who sells appointments to "the client"; Orn Free Taa of Ryloth, a crook; and Lott Dod of the Trade Federation, who took the Damask money.

Each is cleared by *Look into* (Shadow Investigation, 1 capital), by a Bureau intelligence review (it clears the next one on the list), by arresting the Vice Chair (Civil Service track), or, for Free Taa and Dod, by a successful Labyrinth raid. Palpatine is never on the shortlist: he is the one man nobody suspects, and the Caucus cannot point its investigators at him until the trail reaches him. Before the name, investigations speak only of the people who profited, the Senate's inner circle, or the Sith.

**Mas Amedda's files.** Cleared by investigation rather than by arrest, the Vice Chair's dealings can be leaked. He loses the gavel, and the Senate's staff, who resented him, warm to the Caucus. But the Sith Lord learns how close we are: his alarm rises to *He knows we are close*.

**The name.** It opens the charges, the case and *The Hunt for Darth Sidious*, a Caucus Affairs card. Each operation runs once, apart from building the case:

• *Turn Sate Pestage* (once he is cleared): every scheme is foreseen, and any arrest is likelier.

• *Freeze the Damask accounts* (Finance chair or the banking audit): no more bought votes, Black Sun leaves him, and his plan slows.

• *Map compromised officers* (Security chair): Navy loyalty rises, and an unreformed Senate Guard splits.

• *Find the clone order* (the clone army, Kamino known): Order 66 is found, and the chips can be removed.

• *Watch ways off Coruscant*: he cannot flee to the Confederacy, at any point.

• *Bring the name to the Jedi* (relations 45+): their suspicion reaches 9, and they will act with the Senate.

• *Protect the witnesses*: the proof stops wasting away, and no more investigators are assassinated.

• *Tell Anakin the truth* (through Padmé or close Jedi friends): if he is still in the light, he stands with us, and it counts as a warning that was heard; if he has slid far, he goes to Palpatine.

• *Build the public case* (repeatable): more proof.

**His alarm.** Risky operations, leaks and Amedda's fall raise it. At 5 (*He knows we are close*) his plan advances, his next scheme comes at once, and his final move comes a year sooner. At 8 (*He is moving*) he strikes the next month.

**The Chancellor's arrest.** A Caucus Chancellor can order him arrested once.

• *Flight.* If the Republic is at war with the Confederacy and the ways off Coruscant are not watched, he may flee to it. The chance rises with his alarm and with the Confederacy's success.

• *The arrest itself.* For it count our Chancellery's Senate Guard, a warrant, Judicial Forces of 4+, the Jedi with us, conclusive proof, an informant, and Anakin in the light (+1.5). Against it count a fallen Anakin (−2), his alarm and a plan near completion. The balance of forces counts as at the Last Days arrest: +1 if we match him, down to −3 if he has three times our strength.

• *The outcome.* Taken, he stands trial, and the Last Days decide what comes after: the Restoration vote, the war, perhaps his contingency order. Failing that, he fights the arrest, and the battle for Coruscant is decided by the balance of forces, as in his coup from outside office.

@lib_separatists
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= The Separatist threat

**The cause grows every year.** From 31 BBY, the Separatist cause gathers pace each year, unless the Caucus deals with what the Rim resents. How far it has done so counts the steps of the Rim track (above all a Rim Assembly, which halts the growth), the Slavery and Syndicates tracks, the Ethics Commission, budgets that put Rim relief first, relief sent to the Rim, the campaign against slavery and warm relations with the Rimward Coalition. A Federal Compact or a peace stops it. The growth raises separatism in the Outer Rim, the Mid Rim and (when the guilds are unhappy) the corporate holdings, swells the Separatist Caucus, and in wartime feeds the droid armies. High Human Culture adds to it. The Forces tab shows whether the cause is gathering strength or held back.

**Palpatine grows too**, fastest where power lies: the Core and the Colonies, then the Mid Rim, the corporate holdings and, slowest, the Outer Rim.

**Named worlds.** While the Confederacy is stronger than the Republic, or a front has fallen to −3 or worse, every quarter of war (and every day of the Last Days) brings a chance of a breakthrough. The weaker the Republic, the likelier it is. Each front has five loyalist worlds, from the edge of the war inward: *Christophsis, Ryloth, Saleucami, Felucia, Mygeeto* on the Outer Rim; *Rodia, Malastare, Bothawui, Kashyyyk, Naboo* on the Hydian Way; *Anaxes, Ord Mantell, Kuat, Corellia, Alderaan* on the Core approaches. A fallen world raises fear, erodes faith in the Republic and public trust in the Jedi (more the nearer it is to the Core), pushes its front back and takes its region's delegations out of the Senate. When the Republic is clearly stronger and a front is advancing, it can retake the last world lost there. If Alderaan falls, Coruscant is next, and the war is lost.

**The Republic's strength** is the clone army, the Judicial Forces, the member worlds' forces, the Jedi and the Navy's loyalty, with more from a Senate Command Act, a New Jedi Order and a deal with the Hutts. **The Confederacy's** is its droid armies and the guilds' embargo.

**The Sith Lord's hand.** While Palpatine rules the Republic, he keeps the war long but not lost. Out of office, he no longer holds the droid armies back, and strength counts for more. Once he is captured or killed, strength alone decides. **How the war stands**, on the Forces tab, weighs the front, the two sides' strength and the worlds taken; an event warns when the front gives way.

**Two endings belong to the Separatists.** If Sidious is exposed or escapes arrest while the war is going against the Republic, or is beaten on Coruscant in those days, he may flee to the Confederacy and take it over: *The Sith Confederacy*, unless the Republic is clearly winning when the game ends. And if the war is being lost at the end, even with Sidious captured or dead, the Separatists take the Republic: *The Fall of the Republic*.

@lib_defections
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= Shifting blocs and defections

**The centre bleeds.** Over the game the Core Moderates and the Independents each lose about half their natural support. The Caucus and Palpatine share what they lose: more comes to us with good relations with the Moderates, momentum, public evidence against the Sith Lord and our reforms; more goes to him while he is Chancellor and when corruption and fear are high. The Independents also lose voters to the Separatists where separatism runs high, and to the Rimward Coalition where the Rim is suffering. The **Security Bloc** bleeds to Palpatine while he holds office, while fear is high and as his plan advances. When he is unmasked, those who drifted to him vote by their own lights again.

**Four groups can change sides:**

• *The Rimward Coalition* goes over to the Separatists when Ardana leads it and the Rim's separatism has passed 40 (or the Rim's politicians have reached the last stage of radicalisation); or, under any leader, when separatism has passed 55, its relations with us are cold and it is warm toward the Separatists.

• *The Rim Freedom Caucus*, if our Rimward wing has split away, goes over to the Separatists under Tandeth or Corvane once separatism passes 45, or at the last stage of radicalisation.

• *Our own Rimward wing* goes over to the Separatists when Corvane leads it and either its radicalism is 35 or more and separatism has passed 50, or the Rim's politicians have reached the last stage of radicalisation. Farr and Chuchi leave with it.

• *The Security Bloc* folds into Palpatine's bloc when Tarkin leads it, its relations with us are cold and Palpatine is in power, fear is 45 or more, or his plan is well advanced. The Navy's loyalty to the Senate falls with it.

**A warning comes first**, and the group's next leadership challenge comes early. There is no price to pay to stop it. Within four turns, one of these must happen, or the group goes:

• relations with the bloc reach *cordial* (for our own wing: its radicalism falls by 12, or below 30);

• the Caucus takes a step on a track that matters to it: the Rim, Slavery or Syndicates tracks for the Rimward groups, the Military track for the Security Bloc;

• a rival we backed replaces its hardline leader in the challenge.

A threat that is headed off, or that passes because the conditions eased, leaves ten turns of calm.

**A bloc that changes sides** is gone for good. Its support goes to the bloc that absorbed it. If that is the Separatists after Geonosis, its delegations leave the Republic, and the droid armies grow. Its committee chairs are reassigned; a government it belonged to loses it, and a Chancellor from it falls.

@lib_tracks
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= Reforms in steps

Many reforms come in steps. Each step needs the one before it and the right committee chair, and most need a Senate vote. A step taken brings its card back after a single turn. There are six committees: **Finance & Commerce**, **Judiciary & Intelligence** (the investigation office), **Security & Defence** (the military office), **Outer Rim Affairs** (the Rim office), **Constitutional Affairs** and **Jedi Affairs**. Every new government shares the chairs out afresh: each partner insists on at least one, and the Caucus buys the rest with leverage from its seats (Finance 20; Judiciary, Security and the Rim office 10 each; Constitutional and Jedi Affairs 5 each).

Four committees also hand out patronage, six kinds each. A committee's card offers one kind at a time, chosen at random whenever the card is played and never the same twice running:

• *Finance & Commerce:* hyperlane subsidies (the Mid Rim), bond fees for Core banks (the Core), the Corellian Run's customs posts (the Colonies), price supports for agriworlds (the Mid Rim), spaceport credits (the Outer Rim) and freight contracts (the Colonies).

• *Judiciary & Intelligence:* Coruscant's judgeships (the Core), closed investigations (the Colonies), listening posts at inflated rents (the Mid Rim), sector magistracies (the Outer Rim), dropped charges against shipping magnates (the Mid Rim) and intelligence contracts (the Core).

• *Security & Defence:* warship contracts for Fondor's yards (the Colonies), new Judicial bases (the Mid Rim), blasters from Core armsmakers (the Core), militias on the payroll (the Outer Rim), fuel contracts (the Outer Rim) and Judicial commissions (the Colonies).

• *Outer Rim Affairs:* relief contracts through friendly governors, hyperspace beacons and recognised claimants (the Outer Rim), colonisation charters and refugee grants (the Mid Rim), and mining charters for Core firms (the Core).

Each buys political capital and support in its region, at a cost in corruption and faith. Any bloc chairing one of those committees uses it when nothing else it cares about is on offer, unless it opposes corruption, and *The Committees Report* names the kind. Expelling the corrupt senators, the corruption track's last step, ends patronage for good.

**Ministers.** Each chair is held by a named senator of the bloc that holds it. The Caucus chooses its own from its senators (each belongs to a faction, which is pleased to see one of its own chosen); other blocs appoint their own. Every three turns, each committee the Caucus does not chair acts on its bloc's ideology at the time: the bloc's own leanings, plus its current leader's views on regulation, the Rim, emergency powers, the army, peace and oversight. Each committee chooses the act that fits that ideology best, or does nothing if none does. Some acts are reforms put to the Senate (the Core Moderates and the Independents put reforms forward only under their reforming wings, Fang Zar and Satine Kryze), which can fail (a failed bill waits three years before it is tried again); none repeal a reform. When a leader changes, so does the committee's course.

• **The guilds** (Finance & Commerce). 1: regulate the trade guilds. 2: the **Anti-Cartel Act**. 3: **break up the Trade Federation**: the guilds' bloc loses half its support, and their escalation, embargo and threats end for good. The committee can also **cut regulation**, as often as it likes: 1 from the treasury and support on the Rim for a large gain with the guilds and in trade, and each time corporate donors give the Caucus 2 political capital, with a little more corruption. After three rounds, the Caucus can **call on corporate security** in the Last Days, which adds to our side of the balance of forces.

• **The banks** (Finance & Commerce). 1: an audit of the Banking Clan. 2: the Banking Clan's **independence** from the Chancellery. 3: **Republic bonds**: the Republic's interest falls by nearly a third.

• **The bureaucracy** (investigation office). 1: reform the Senate bureaucracy. 2: the **Civil Service Act**. After it, the bureaucrats no longer build cases against us, purge our allies or lose our bills in committee, and the Caucus can **arrest Mas Amedda** (with some evidence): more evidence, more support everywhere, and an end to the Vice Chair's gavel.

• **The Rim** (Rim office). 1: the **Rim Relief Act**. 2: enfranchisement (or a constitutional amendment). 3: a **Rim Assembly**, which needs warm relations with the Rimward Coalition and halts the Separatists' growth.

• **Corruption** (investigation office). 1: the Ethics Commission. 2: the **Office of Public Integrity**: corruption falls faster and votes can no longer be bought. 3: **expel the Senate's most corrupt members**: corruption falls sharply and faith rises, but the guilds, Palpatine's bloc and the Rimward Coalition lose senators and resent it.

• **Intelligence** (investigation office). 1: an intelligence review. 2: the **Intelligence Oversight Act**: the Bureau of Intelligence answers to the Senate's committee. 3: the **Commission for the Protection of the Republic**: schemes are foreseen, proof stops wasting away, and no more investigators are assassinated.

• **Emergency powers** (Constitutional Affairs). 1: sunset clauses. 2: the **Powers Review Amendment**: every year the Senate takes one power back.

• **The office of Chancellor** (Constitutional Affairs). Entrenched term limits and a constructive vote of no confidence, in either order, then an **independent Speaker**: the Chancellor no longer presides over the Senate, and the Vice Chair's gavel can no longer table our bills.

• **The military** (military office). 1: Senate oversight of the army. 2: the **Senate Command Act**, once the Senate Guard is reformed: the clones and the Navy look to the Senate. It does not rule out the admirals.

• **The Senate's guard** (military office). 1: return the Senate Guard to the Senate. 2: the **Judicial Forces Charter**: two more divisions of Judicial Forces, answering to the courts.

• **The Order's future** (Jedi Affairs). 1: speak for reform, three times (the Caucus's Jedi card can do it too). 2: the Council accepts attachment (or blesses Anakin's marriage). 3: **a New Jedi Order** of families, Knights who come as adults, no Jedi generals and an open Council. It needs Anakin to choose the light (once found, if he has not slid far toward the dark and the Council has accepted attachment) and close relations with the Order. Anakin can then no longer fall.

• **The Order's independence** (Jedi Affairs). 1: the **Jedi Independence Amendment**: the Chancellor can never place the Council under his authority. 2: the **Jedi Service Act**: the Jedi serve as peacekeepers and advisers, never as generals; the Order recovers, and the war is harder. It is a standing law: passed in peacetime, it keeps the Jedi out of command when war comes. *Generals of the Republic* then becomes a motion to suspend it for the duration of the war, which the Caucus can fight in a Senate vote or let pass. Suspended, it returns in force when the war ends, and the Caucus can pass it again sooner. 3: a **Senate–Jedi Compact**: the Council and the Senate act together.

• **Slavery** (Rim office). 1: a **Senate report on slavery**. 2: abolition. 3: **enforcing abolition**, once the syndicates are broken, which frees Shmi Skywalker if she still lives. Every step raises Caucus support (most in the Rim) and draws Anakin back from the dark.

• **The syndicates** (military office, then investigation office). 1: **patrol fleets** for the Rim, once the Republic has invested in its military at least once (raising the Judicial Forces, Navy loyalty work, training or exercising the Defence Pact, a defence budget of our own). Pirate raids stop. 2: a **campaign against the pirate fleets**: with fewer than two investments it ends in a humiliating defeat. 3: **break the syndicates**: a raid that may fail, likelier to succeed with stronger Judicial Forces, more evidence and the integrity office, and with each try. Corruption falls sharply and Caucus support rises.

• **High Human Culture**. 1: the **Rights of Sentience** (Rim office). 2: **diversify the officer corps** (military office). 3: **investigate the human-first groups** (investigation office). 4: once all three are done, the movement is exposed and discredited.

@lib_shadows
is-special: true
new-page: true
go-to: menu

= Guild money, the syndicates and High Human Culture

**Guild money has strings.** Taking the guilds' money, in Patron Contributions or as a war chest in a Chancellor race (offered when the guilds are content and there is no integrity office), adds a debt. While we owe them, corruption creeps up and the guilds block the reforms that would hurt them: the Ethics Commission and the integrity office, regulating the guilds, breaking up the Trade Federation, Banking Clan independence, the trade tax and tariffs in budgets we write. **Breaking with the guilds** (Patron Contributions) clears every debt, for 2 political capital and the guilds' anger; the Rimward and Pacifist wings approve, and the guilds may leak what we took.

**Pirates.** Until patrol fleets guard the Rim, pirates raid the trade lanes now and then, more often as the years pass. Each raid costs support for the Chancellor's bloc in the Rim and the Colonies, cuts trade and drives voters to the Security Bloc.

**A deal with the Hutts.** In wartime, while the syndicates stand, the Hutt clans offer their hyperspace lanes. The government decides, unless the Caucus commands the war; otherwise our choice needs a Senate majority. Taking the deal gives the Outer Rim front a great push, strengthens the Republic and lifts trade, but corruption leaps and the Syndicates track can never be finished.

**Black Sun.** If the syndicates still stand when Palpatine makes his move, Black Sun's enforcers fight for him on Coruscant. Each step on the Syndicates track weakens them; breaking the syndicates removes them.

**High Human Culture.** Until it is discredited, the human-first movement shows itself every few turns, on Coruscant's streets, in the Senate and in the officer corps. Each time, Palpatine gains in the Core and the Colonies, the Separatist cause gathers pace, and hardline leaders find it easier to win leadership contests; in the officer corps, the Navy's loyalty to the Senate slips. Discrediting it ends all of this, and takes away one of the things the admirals need to strike after Sidious falls.

